<p>A puzzle from Alcuin of York&#8217;s <em>Propositiones ad Acuendos Juvenes</em> (Problems to Sharpen the Young), 9th century:</p>
<p>&#8220;A man and his wife, each the weight of a loaded cart, who had two children each the weight of a small cart, needed to cross a river. However, the boat they came across could only carry the weight of one cart. Let him devise [a way] of crossing in order that the boat should not sink.&#8221;</p>
<p>(The boat can bear the weight of two children or one adult.)</p>

